	PROFACAD_CH_KNOWLEDGE_TITLE = "What is Knowledge?",
	PROFACAD_CH_KNOWLEDGE_INTRO = "Before anything else: a profession has two numbers. Skill rises just by crafting. Knowledge is the one that matters — it is limited, mostly weekly, and it decides what you can make. This chapter is about the second one.",
	PROFACAD_CH_KNOWLEDGE_BODY = "Your profession grows in two ways, and that is exactly where the confusion sits.\n\nYour |cffffffffSkill|r rises by making things. That happens by itself. Alongside it you collect |cffffffffKnowledge Points|r, which you do not get from crafting at all but from orders, treasures and books — and you spend those in a tree, where you choose what you become good at.\n\nThe image that helps: |cffffffffskill is how often you have done it, knowledge is what you have gone deep on.|r Two alchemists at the same skill can make completely different things, because they put their knowledge somewhere else. That is also why you cannot have everything. You have to choose.\n\n|cffffcc00Where the points come from — five sources, and the first dwarfs the rest.|r\n\n1. Orders from the game itself (Patron Orders), at your own workbench. Its own chapter follows, including the trap about doing them all at the start of the week.\n2. Crafting everything once. Every recipe's first craft is a point. Do this before you start spending, so you have more to work with.\n3. Treasures out in the world. Scattered across the Midnight zones are containers that grant points — a decent haul if you have not collected them yet.\n4. A weekly quest at your trainer, and a treatise a scribe can make for you.\n5. Reputation books. A few factions will sell you a book that grants ten at once.\n\nCooking, Fishing and Archaeology have no trees and need no points at all.\n\nDo not worry about being \"behind\": missed weeks come back slowly through a catch-up system, and this course walks you through it one step at a time.",
	PROFACAD_CH_KNOWLEDGE_TASK = "Open your profession window (default key: K) and have a look at the Specializations tab.",
	PROFACAD_CH_GEARUP_TITLE = "Gear up: tools, stations & first recipes",
	PROFACAD_CH_GEARUP_INTRO = "Professions are split per expansion, so the window can look empty when it is really just showing the wrong era. Step one is making sure you are looking at Midnight.",
	PROFACAD_CH_GEARUP_BODY = "First things first: the dropdown at the top of the profession window switches expansion tiers — everything in this course is about the MIDNIGHT tier. Don't see it in the list? Learn it from the Silvermoon trainers.\n\nEvery profession also has its own equipment — 1 tool and 2 accessories — in special slots at the top of the profession window (K). Without a tool equipped, many recipes simply won't craft. Most tools and accessories are crafted by OTHER professions (Blacksmithing, Leatherworking) — buy them on the Auction House under Profession Equipment, or ask a crafter; the supplies vendor sometimes stocks basics. Enchanting is special and crafts its own rods: buy the cheap base rod from the vendor, craft the Runed version at the Enchanter's Lectern (skill 1 trainer recipe), equip it.\n\nGreyed-out craft button? Read the requirement line in the recipe: it tells you whether you're missing a tool or need to stand at a station (anvil, lectern, cooking fire...). The stations are around the trainer district in Silvermoon.\n\nThen learn your first recipes at the trainer and craft them — skill 25 unlocks your specialization trees, and every first craft gives +1 KP.",
	PROFACAD_CH_GEARUP_TASK = "Equip a tool for each of your professions.",
	PROFACAD_CH_TREES_TITLE = "Choosing trees — without regret",
	PROFACAD_CH_TREES_INTRO = "Your specialization tree is where Knowledge goes. Think of it as a talent tree for your craft: what you put in decides what you can make, and how well.",
	PROFACAD_CH_TREES_ADVANCED = "Where do you actually click? Open your profession window (the one with your recipes), then look at the tabs along the bottom: Recipes, Specializations, Crafting Orders. Specializations is the one — that is where your trees live and where Knowledge is spent. Nothing happens until you confirm, so it is safe to open it and just look around first. If you are not sure which tree, come back here: the Professions overview names the next node for you whenever it can read your trees.",
	PROFACAD_CH_TREES_BODY = "Three or four |cffffffffwheels|r sit side by side there. Each is built from round slots joined by lines, and you can tell one by the ring around it that fills as you put points in — hover it and you will see something like \"3 / 30\", meaning three points in and thirty is full. Guides call these slots nodes. Every wheel has one big slot in the middle (the root) and smaller ones around it that only appear once the middle one has enough in it. Looking around costs nothing: nothing changes until you confirm.\n\n|cffffcc00The one rule that matters: do not spread your points.|r\n\nPutting a little into everything feels clever. It is the most expensive mistake there is, and everybody makes it. The reason is that everything worth having sits behind a |cffffffffthreshold|r. At thirty points something happens; at fifteen nothing does. Four half-filled slots therefore give you |cffffffffnothing|r — you have spent a hundred points and still cannot do anything special.\n\nPick one. Finish it. Then move on. How large a threshold is differs per profession and even per slot, so do not go by a number from a guide: hover the slot and it tells you.\n\n|cffffcc00What you get for opening a branch|r is not what most people expect, and it is good news. The recipe usually comes from |cffffffffopening|r a branch rather than filling it — you open it and you can make the thing straight away. The points you put in afterwards do not make it possible, they make it |cffffffffbetter|r. Opening costs you no Knowledge Point either; you get separate unlocks for that at fixed points in the middle slot. But you do not get unlimited ones. To open a second branch you must first go deeper in the middle. Opening is free, but rationed.\n\n|cffffcc00And some things are simply switched off,|r which is the trap where people conclude the game is broken. A few branches turn on something that did not exist before. An engineer can recycle old gear, but only |cffffffffdiscovers recipes|r from it after putting points in — without that you recycle forever and nothing happens. A tailor only sees the expensive cloth drop once the matching branch is learned. A herbalist can only pick from a mount after going deep enough. If something \"does not seem to work\", check whether it still has to be switched on.\n\n|cffffcc00You get one do-over.|r Since patch 12.1 you can undo your points |cffffffffonce per profession|r, at Theremis in Silvermoon, near the order table. Once — after that your choices stand. It also unlearns the recipes those points had unlocked; they come back when you place the points again, but do not let it startle you. And you do not need to be nervous about it: keep collecting and you can eventually fill every branch, so points cannot really be |cffffffffwasted|r — only be in the wrong place during the months it matters.\n\nYou get a choice of tree at skill 25, 50, 60 and 75. The game does not decide which; you do. Once you have professions, their own chapters below give a concrete starter build, and the Professions overview shows live advice for your own trees.",
	PROFACAD_CH_TREES_TASK = "Look at your trees and decide on ONE first goal — don't spend anything yet.",
	PROFACAD_CH_QUALITY_TITLE = "Quality — why his sword is better than yours",
	PROFACAD_CH_QUALITY_INTRO = "Two players use the same recipe and one result comes out better. That is not bad luck and not a bug: crafted things have a quality rank.",
	PROFACAD_CH_QUALITY_ADVANCED = "Where do you actually click? Open a recipe in your profession window and look at the Crafting Details box on the right: it shows Recipe Difficulty against your Skill, which is the whole calculation in two numbers. Click any reagent in the recipe and you can choose which quality of material goes in — the tooltip says \"Left Click to allocate reagent qualities\". There is also a \"Use Best Quality Reagents\" tick that picks your best automatically. Handy, but it spends your expensive materials without asking twice.",
	PROFACAD_CH_QUALITY_BODY = "The image that helps: the recipe says |cffffffffwhat|r you are making, the quality says how well it turned out. Two bakers with the same recipe do not bake the same bread.\n\nYou read it off a small |cffffffffshape|r rather than a number. The tooltip says \"Quality:\" with the shape beside it — a grey diamond is low, a golden pentagon is high. In your profession window the same thing appears as a bar, lowest shape on the left and highest on the right, with a white marker showing roughly where your craft will land.\n\nIt matters for three reasons, and the third one catches everyone once. Better gear and stronger potions, obviously. More gold: the same recipe, Scalewoven Hide, sold for 10g94 at the lowest rank and 21g12 at the highest — nearly double, for the same materials and the same click. Each rank is genuinely a |cffffffffseparate item|r with its own auction listing, so they do not stack together. And the third: if someone orders a minimum quality you cannot reach, you |cffffffffcannot complete that order at all|r. Not \"it turns out worse\" — you literally cannot hand it in.\n\nWhat decides it is one number: your |cffffffffSkill|r at the moment you press craft. Every recipe has a difficulty. Comfortably above it and you get the top rank; below it and you do not.\n\nThat number is built from four things. Your base skill, which rises as you craft and eventually fills up. Your Knowledge Points, which add skill to |cffffffffthat kind of recipe only|r — points in rings make your rings better, not your necklaces. Your profession gear: a tool and two accessories per profession, in their own equipment slots, which a great many people simply leave empty. And your reagents, which carry the same quality shapes, so cheap materials are not always cheaper.\n\nIf you land one step short there is a safety net, called Concentration. It has its own chapter below.\n\nOne trap worth naming, because it costs real gold: |cffffffffbeing half specialised is more expensive than not specialising at all|r. Spread your points over four branches and you fall short everywhere. You can make anything at a mediocre rank, and mediocre does not sell and gets refused by orders.\n\nIf you gather rather than craft, your herbs and ore carry ranks too — but only |cfffffffftwo|r of them in Midnight, not the full ladder. More skill means the better of the two more often. There is no third rank to chase.",
	PROFACAD_CH_QUALITY_TASK = "Open a recipe and find the Quality bar and the Crafting Details box.",
	PROFACAD_CH_STATS_TITLE = "The six numbers nobody explains",
	PROFACAD_CH_STATS_INTRO = "Multicraft, Resourcefulness, Deftness. The game never says what they do, and most players look them up again every single time.",
	PROFACAD_CH_STATS_ADVANCED = "Where do you actually see them? Open any recipe in your profession window and look at the Crafting Details box on the right — the six appear there as percentages, for example Resourcefulness 4%, Multicraft 4%, Ingenuity 19%. The same box shows Recipe Difficulty against your Skill, and below that, what Concentration would cost you to force the next quality rank.",
	PROFACAD_CH_STATS_BODY = "The image that helps: |cffffffffSkill decides how good it turns out, these six decide how cheaply you got there.|r Skill is about the result; these are about the road to it.\n\nThere are three for crafting and three for gathering, so you never need all six at once.\n\nCrafting. |cffffffffResourcefulness|r means a craft sometimes consumes less than it should and you keep the materials. It works on everything, and it is the only one that improves your profit on someone else's order, because the leftovers stay with you rather than the customer. |cffffffffMulticraft|r means you sometimes get more than one out of the same materials. |cffffffffIngenuity|r gives you part of your Concentration back.\n\nThe trap in that list: |cffffffffMulticraft does nothing for equipment.|r An extra copy only means something for things that stack — potions, gems, materials. A ring or a breastplate never doubles, so if you mainly craft gear, Multicraft is wasted. And on other people's orders the extra copies go to the |cffffffffcustomer|r, not to you. That last part is what players report to each other; Blizzard has never confirmed it.\n\nGathering. |cffffffffFinesse|r gives you more per node, which is your volume and therefore your gold. |cffffffffPerception|r gives you a better chance at the rare material rather than more of the ordinary one. |cffffffffDeftness|r makes the gathering animation shorter.\n\nWhich one matters depends on what you do, and that is the useful part. Herbalism wants Finesse: per hour you spend far more time finding and flying than picking, so gathering faster solves the wrong problem. Mining is the same, and Perception is weak there, because the rare ore it steers you toward has little use in recipes. |cffffffffSkinning inverts it|r — once you are hunting the rare beasts, Perception is strong, because that is where the expensive drops are. It is the one gathering profession where that holds. Those three are judgements about how the professions play rather than numbers anyone has published.\n\nThe values come from two places you control: your Knowledge Points, since many nodes grant one of these six, and your profession gear. So when you spend a point, look at which stat sits under the node and whether it matches what you actually intend to make.\n\nIf you remember one thing: |cffffffffdecide what you are going to make, then pick your stat.|r Someone selling potions and someone making rings have opposite interests, and a guide that says \"Multicraft is best\" is not telling you who it is talking to.",
	PROFACAD_CH_STATS_TASK = "Open Crafting Details on a recipe and read your own three percentages.",
	PROFACAD_CH_CONC_TITLE = "Concentration, the emergency brake",
	PROFACAD_CH_CONC_INTRO = "Sometimes you land one rank short, and making the thing anyway feels like a waste. That is exactly what Concentration is for.",
	PROFACAD_CH_CONC_ADVANCED = "Where do you actually find it? In your profession window it is a bar near the top showing your current total, and when you open a recipe there is a flame button beside the craft button showing what that particular craft would cost. The cost differs per recipe, so read it before you press.",
	PROFACAD_CH_CONC_BODY = "The image: |cffffffffit is a battery that fills itself.|r You do nothing for it, it refills on its own, and you can drain it at the moment it matters. Spend it on a craft and the next quality rank is |cffffffffguaranteed|r — not a chance, not a roll. The game says so itself: \"Apply 358 concentration to guarantee the next quality for this recipe.\"\n\nHere is the whole story, and it is why people misuse it. The battery holds |cffffffff1000|r and refills at about |cffffffff250 a day|r, so roughly four days from empty to full. That is slow. Every time you use it you are spending part of your next few days, and when it is empty you can only wait.\n\nEach profession has its |cffffffffown|r battery. An alchemist who also picks herbs has two, and they are unrelated.\n\nIt is not cheap per use, either. One real craft cost 358 of the 1000 — more than a third of the battery for a single item.\n\nWorth spending on: something one rank short that you will wear or use yourself; an order with a minimum quality you cannot otherwise reach, since without it you cannot complete that order at all; and anything expensive, where the gap between ranks is worth more than a day of battery.\n\nNot worth spending on: something you already make at top rank anyway, where you buy nothing and lose the battery. That is the most common mistake, because people leave the button switched on and never notice. And cheap bulk — fifty potions one rank higher is pleasant, but it is not four days.\n\nYou get some of it back. |cffffffffIngenuity|r, one of the six stats, occasionally refunds part of what you just spent, and the game tells you when it happens. That is exactly why Ingenuity only becomes interesting once you are steering for quality: with nothing spent, there is nothing to refund.\n\nOne more thing, which is about people rather than mechanics. If you burn Concentration to hit the quality on |cffffffffsomeone else's|r order, they get the better item and you pay from a battery that takes days to recover. Experienced crafters charge more for that. It is not a rule and there is no set rate, but it is worth knowing that asking is not greedy and being asked is not strange.\n\nConcentration is not a system to manage or optimise. It is a brake, there for the moment you are one rank short of something worth it. If it fills up while you play, you are doing fine. If it sits at full for weeks, you are probably leaving quality on the table.",
	PROFACAD_CH_CONC_TASK = "Find your Concentration bar and check whether it has been sitting at full.",
	PROFACAD_CH_GOLD_TITLE = "Making gold without fooling yourself",
	PROFACAD_CH_GOLD_INTRO = "There is only one sum here, and almost nobody does it: what you are paid, minus everything that went in, minus 5% for the auction house.",
	PROFACAD_CH_GOLD_BODY = "The word people skip is |cffffffffeverything|r. Herbs you picked yourself are |cffffffffnot free|r. They have a market price, and putting them into a potion instead of selling them means you spent it — you just paid it to yourself. If the herb is worth more than the potion, crafting is |cfffffffflosing money in a way that feels like earning it|r.\n\n|cffffcc00There are two markets, and they obey opposite laws.|r Almost all confusion about whether a profession \"pays\" comes from mixing them up.\n\nThe first is |cffffffffgoods|r: materials, potions, flasks, gems — anything that stacks. You sell things, and you compete with the whole region rather than your own realm. Buyers see nothing but the price.\n\nThe second is |cffffffffcrafted gear|r. It stays on your realm, and the best of it binds to one person, so it cannot go on the auction house at all. Here you are not selling things, you are selling your craftsmanship.\n\n|cffffcc00Why the goods market always sinks|r is worth understanding, and none of the reasons is \"lots of players\". The game buys the cheapest listing automatically, so one copper above the lowest price means no sale. You compete against every realm in your region at once. Multicraft hands out free extra supply, and it works only on things that stack — precisely this market. And consumables have only two quality ranks rather than the full ladder, so it is a shorter fall to the bottom.\n\nThere is one brake: while crafters still need Concentration to reach top quality, top quality is scarce. Once they can hit it unaided the brake lets go and the price falls. So in this market you earn in the window |cffffffffbefore|r that happens.\n\n|cffffcc00What actually protects a price is a lock on supply.|r There are four kinds: a cooldown on the recipe, a reagent the crafter can never supply themselves, a recipe behind reputation, and Concentration, because it refills slowly. |cffffffffWhere there is a lock there is margin. Where there is none there is volume and almost no margin.|r That is the whole subject in one sentence.\n\nGathering is the exception that proves it: what you pick has no cost, so you |cffffffffcannot|r lose — the only way of making gold where a beginner cannot go wrong. But no cost also means no barrier, so the price settles at whatever the least fussy gatherer will accept.\n\n|cffffcc00Every crafted item falls into one of three boxes, and the third is the trap.|r Freely tradable says so in its own tooltip: \"Can be bought and sold on the Auction House.\" Soulbound cannot be auctioned but |cffffffffcan|r be sold through an order, which delivers straight to the customer — there you are selling labour, not a product. And |cffffffffBinds to Warband|r can do neither: no auction, no order, only you and your own alts. That is not a business, it is a service.\n\nAlchemists especially: the two cauldrons that supply your whole raid are |cffffffffboth|r Binds to Warband. You can never sell them. You make them, you bring them, and what you get back is goodwill. That is fine — but it should be a choice rather than a discovery, because expensive materials go in.\n\n|cffffcc00Bulk or made to order?|r In bulk you sell the product, compete with the whole region on price, have unlimited volume and a margin that keeps thinning. On orders you sell your skill, compete with crafters on your own realm on reputation, have limited volume and a steadier margin. From which follows an arithmetic rather than an opinion: |cffffffffthe public order queue is not a business.|r You hold four and get one back a day — about seven a week. That is pocket money. |cffffffffPersonal and guild orders are unlimited|r, so a handful of regulars who send you orders directly is where the real work is.\n\n|cffffcc00The biggest beginner mistake|r is crafting before checking what the materials cost. It wears two faces and they are the same error: \"I picked it myself, so it is free\" and \"I have this recipe, so I will make it\". Having a recipe says nothing about whether it pays. |cffffffffCheck what it costs first. Then decide whether to make it.|r\n\nAnd distrust every \"X gold per hour\" from a guide or a video: it differs per realm and is usually invented. Measure on your own realm instead. Auctionator shows what things really cost; CraftSim works out what a craft earns against today's material prices, and is the single most useful thing to install if you want to earn seriously.",
	PROFACAD_CH_GOLD_DATED_202608 = "|cffffcc00How it stands in late August 2026 — and this half expires.|r Season 2 is days old, so treat the paragraphs below as a snapshot rather than a rule. Worth knowing: nearly every \"Season 2\" guide out there is dated before the season even started, which makes their claims predictions rather than observations. No published price series exists for these first weeks, from anyone.\n\n|cffffffffAlchemy.|r Potions and flasks are freely tradable and selling well while the raid is new and demand is peaking — but this is the goods market in full flow, so expect the margin to sink as more crafters reach top quality without Concentration. Wondrous Synergist is the steadier income: it carries a cooldown of roughly a day, so nobody can flood the market, which is exactly the lock described above, and it feeds both cauldrons and the epic stone. Housing decoration is the underrated one — six recipes, tradable, and it does not stack, so it competes on your own realm instead of the regional pool. One market story worth carrying: 12.1's new health potion consumes 25 of the old one, so the old potion was promoted from finished product to ingredient and roughly halved in value as a product. Anyone who stockpiled it in July met that halving on 11 August.\n\n|cffffffffHerbalism.|r Nocturnal Lotus is the expensive herb, and the reason is precise: it is the only Midnight herb of rare quality, it has no node of its own (it turns up as a by-catch of every other herb), and it appears in all four flasks and both cauldrons — eleven recipes in total. The two new materials from The Coiled Isle are both tradable, with one warning: they are |cffffffffnot|r exclusive to herbalists. Miners pull them from their own nodes, and one of them also drops from animals through Skinning. Several guides imply otherwise.",
	PROFACAD_CH_GOLD_TASK = "Before your next craft, look up what its materials cost — and only then decide to make it.",
	PROFACAD_CH_RECIPES_TITLE = "Where do recipes come from?",
	PROFACAD_CH_RECIPES_INTRO = "Not knowing where recipes come from is the most common reason a profession feels stuck. There are five sources, and only one of them is your trainer.",
	PROFACAD_CH_RECIPES_BODY = "Recipes come from five places: your trainer (buy while leveling skill), your specialization trees (unlock as you spend KP), Moxie vendors (next chapter), renown rewards, and world drops or treasures.\n\nRule of thumb: craft every new recipe at least once. Each first craft gives +1 KP and 10x Moxie — even if you vendor the result.\n\nIf a guide mentions a recipe you don't have, check where it comes from before farming for it.",
	PROFACAD_CH_RECIPES_TASK = "Learn one new recipe and craft it once (first craft = +1 KP + Moxie).",
	PROFACAD_CH_MOXIE_TITLE = "Moxie, your shopping money",
	PROFACAD_CH_MOXIE_INTRO = "Each profession has its own currency, and it only buys things for that profession. Worth knowing before you go shopping with the wrong one.",
	PROFACAD_CH_MOXIE_BODY = "Artisan's Moxie is a profession-specific currency: your Enchanting Moxie only buys Enchanting things. You earn roughly 5x Moxie per KP, and 10x from first crafts.\n\nSpend it at the profession vendors in Silvermoon on new recipes, bags of materials, and books that grant bonus KP.\n\nTip: don't hoard endlessly — early recipes and the KP books are the best value.",
	PROFACAD_CH_MOXIE_TASK = "Find your Moxie in the currency tab and check your balance.",
	PROFACAD_CH_WEEKLY_TITLE = "Your weekly routine",
	PROFACAD_CH_WEEKLY_INTRO = "Most Knowledge comes back every week, which means a missed week is gone for good. This is the routine worth building a habit around.",
	PROFACAD_CH_WEEKLY_BODY = "Most KP comes from repeatable weekly sources. The basics for every profession:\n\n1. Trainer weekly quest — unlocks after the intro quest at Captain Flaresworn (Work Order station, Silvermoon Bazaar). Enchanting needs skill 25 first.\n2. Two random treasure-drop items from open-world containers (+2 KP each).\n3. A Thalassian Treatise (+1 KP) — crafted by Inscription; warbound, so an alt or a work order gets you one.\n4. Darkmoon Faire week: +3 KP and +15 Moxie, monthly.\n\nMissed a week? The catch-up system slowly returns missed KP — after you finish the current weeklies.",
	PROFACAD_CH_WEEKLY_TASK = "Pick up the intro quest at Captain Flaresworn and do your first trainer weekly.",
	PROFACAD_CH_WORKORDERS_TITLE = "Work orders: buying someone's craftsmanship",
	PROFACAD_CH_WORKORDERS_INTRO = "The auction house sells you things that already exist. At the order table it works the other way round: you bring the materials and pay someone for the work.",
	PROFACAD_CH_WORKORDERS_ADVANCED = "Two things that make the system look broken when it is not. At your own workbench, the list of public orders stays empty until you press |cffffffffSearch|r — nothing is wrong, it simply does not load by itself. And the man standing at the order table, Captain Flaresworn, is not part of this at all: he hands out your profession's intro quest, your weekly quests and free bags of materials. Useful, but not what you came for.",
	PROFACAD_CH_WORKORDERS_BODY = "It works this way because the best crafted gear binds to one person the moment it exists. Nobody can make it in advance and put it on the shelf, so someone has to make it |cffffffffspecifically for you|r.\n\n|cffffcc00There are two counters, and this is where it usually goes wrong.|r To have something made, go to the order table in the Silvermoon Bazaar — the big golden hammer on your minimap. The person you need is Mar'nah. To make something for someone else, go to |cffffffffyour own profession's workbench|r instead. If you are not standing next to it there is no button and no list, and it looks as though the system does not exist. That is the most common mistake, and the game explains it nowhere.\n\n|cffffcc00Having something made.|r Pick your item, then pick who you are asking. To nobody in particular (the button says Public Order): everyone on your realm sees it and the first to take it makes it. Fast, but you get what you get — |cffffffffyou cannot demand a quality here|r, and you must send every material yourself. To one specific player (Personal Order): here you may set a minimum quality, and you may agree that they add materials of their own. To your whole guild (Guild Order) works the same way and reaches guildmates on other realms. You set the payment, and a button beside it shows what others recently paid for the same item.\n\nThree things to know before you click order. Some special reagents can never be supplied by the crafter, so you always send those yourself. Materials in your Warband bank |cffffffffdo not count|r — put them in your bags. And the item binds to |cffffffffyou|r, so order on the character who will wear it, not the one holding the gold. Once someone picks the order up you cannot cancel it; the item arrives by mail.\n\n|cffffcc00Making for others.|r At your workbench you see what you will earn before you start. Once you begin you have |cffffffff30 minutes|r to finish — enough to fetch something, not enough to go and eat.\n\nYou cannot take public orders endlessly: you hold |cffffffff four|r, and |cffffffffone comes back each day|r. So roughly seven a week, not four a day. Orders that are nearly expired are free to take.\n\nIf an order refuses you it is one of four things: you do not know the recipe, your specialization is not far enough for the extras it asks for, it wants a quality you cannot reach, or your allowance is spent.\n\nAnd one that quietly costs you money: |cffffffffcheck whose materials you are using.|r On public orders the customer always supplies everything, but on the other kinds they may supply nothing — and then you are cheerfully crafting with your own expensive stock for a tip.",
	PROFACAD_CH_WORKORDERS_TASK = "Find the order table in the Silvermoon Bazaar, then find your own profession's workbench. They are not the same place.",
	PROFACAD_CH_PATRON_TITLE = "Patron orders, your weekly Knowledge engine",
	PROFACAD_CH_PATRON_INTRO = "The fourth kind of order comes from the game itself rather than from a player, and it is the one most people never find.",
	PROFACAD_CH_PATRON_BODY = "At your own workbench there is a |cffffffffPatron|r tab where the game gives you orders. No other player, no competition — they are yours alone. What you are paid is not really gold but |cffffffffKnowledge Points|r: the same points that make your profession stronger.\n\nThis is by far your biggest weekly source of them. Skip it and you fall behind without ever understanding why other people are moving faster.\n\nA fresh batch arrives every few days. Each order asks for a certain quality, and sometimes you have to supply materials yourself — check that, because an expensive order for two points is not always worth it. Skipping one is allowed.\n\n|cffffcc00And now the trap.|r The game also gives you a small one-point order each day, but |cffffffffonly while you are behind your weekly goal|r. Do all the big orders on Monday and you are caught up — so the little daily ones stop for the rest of the week.\n\nSo: |cffffffffleave the big orders until they are close to expiring|r. You keep collecting the small ones in the meantime, and end the week with more points than you would have had. It feels wrong. It is true anyway.",
	PROFACAD_CH_PATRON_TASK = "Open the Patron tab at your own workbench and look at what this week is offering.",
	PROFACAD_CH_ENCHANTING_TITLE = "Enchanting starter guide",
	PROFACAD_CH_ENCHANTING_INTRO = "Enchanting adds permanent stats to your gear, and it is the only profession that turns unwanted loot into progress. Disenchanting simply means destroying a green or blue item for materials. Your first step: disenchant the junk in your bags. The build order below is for when you are comfortable.",
	PROFACAD_CH_ENCHANTING_ADVANCED = "Past the starter build? Once those three roots are in there is no single right next node - it depends on what you want the profession to do. Points in the Weapon/Ring/Chest branch buy the enchants people actually ask for. Points in Disenchanting Delegate mean buying fewer materials yourself. More points in Spellbound Shatterer raise the Shatter Essence buff, so everything you craft comes out a little better. Whichever you pick, spend them: Knowledge sitting unspent does nothing at all, and none of these is a wrong enough choice to be worth saving points over.",
	PROFACAD_CH_ENCHANTING_BODY = "Enchanting is the odd one out: you earn most weekly KP by disenchanting gear. Each week the first 5 Swirling Arcane Essences give +1 KP each, then a Brimming Mana Shard gives +4 — that is +9 KP per week just for shattering loot. Enchanting gets no patron orders.\n\nStarter build (community consensus): Spellbound Shatterer first (its Shatter Essence buff boosts your crafting stats), then Elevating Equipment — root to ~30, then the Weapon/Ring/Chest branch (the most wanted enchants). Disenchanting Delegate is a strong third for material self-sufficiency.\n\nExtra KP books: Silvermoon Court renown 6, and the Abundance event vendor (Chel the Chip) sells an Enchanting book too.",
	PROFACAD_CH_ENCHANTING_TASK = "Disenchant gear until you have this week's 5 Essences + Mana Shard (+9 KP).",
	PROFACAD_CH_ALCHEMY_TITLE = "Alchemy starter guide",
	PROFACAD_CH_ALCHEMY_INTRO = "Alchemy makes the potions and flasks people drink before every boss, so there is always demand. Your first step is simply to keep brewing while your skill climbs.",
	PROFACAD_CH_ALCHEMY_BODY = "Alchemy offers you a tree at skill 25, 50, 60 and 75 — the game does not choose, you do. The four are Potion Prowess (potions, with a Light and a Void side), Fluent in Flasks, Transmutation Authority and Alchemical Mastery.\n\n|cffffcc00Starter build, in this order.|r |cffffffffPotion Prowess|r first: filling it gives the Voidlight Potion Cauldron, which serves your gold and your guild at the same time. Then |cffffffffPath of Light|r and |cffffffffProlific Potioneer - Light|r, which is the Multicraft node — Light because that is the potion that sells this season. Then |cffffffffAlchemical Mastery|r and inside it |cffffffffReuse|r, which hands herbs back to you and is strong if you also gather them.\n\nAfter that about 15 points in |cffffffffFluent in Flasks|r, which doubles how long your own flasks last, then |cffffffffSin'dorei Specialist|r. |cffffffffTransmutation Authority|r comes last, not second.\n\n|cffffffffHaranir Secrets is not an afterthought.|r Its own tooltip ends with \"and Cauldron of Sin'dorei Flasks\" — without points there you cannot make the flask cauldron at top rank. Guides paraphrase that node as \"phials only\" and drop the half-sentence that matters.",
	PROFACAD_CH_ALCHEMY_TASK = "Do your available Patron Orders this week (crafting order board).",
	PROFACAD_CH_TAILORING_TITLE = "Tailoring starter guide",
	PROFACAD_CH_TAILORING_INTRO = "If you wear cloth, tailoring makes your gear — and the cloth every other crafter needs. Your first step is picking up the cloth you already walk past.",
	PROFACAD_CH_TAILORING_BODY = "Tailors are the only source of crafted cloth armor — and of the bolts (Arcanoweave and Sunfire Silk) that feed bags, embellished gear and spellthreads. Those bolts sit behind a daily cooldown that only specialized tailors unlock: reason enough to spec deliberately.\n\nStarter build: put your first ~30 points in the Nimble Needlework root (skill and stats for armor AND bolts), then pick your focus. Armor: Sin'dorei Finery — choose ONE slot group (e.g. robes/trousers/cloaks) and max it before starting another. Bolts/gold: Sunfire Silk Weaving or Arcanoweaving plus its Expertise node (more bolts, shorter cooldown).\n\nFiber Arts and Fabric Specialist improve every craft (and your cloth drops in specific zones) — solid filler for spare points.",
	PROFACAD_CH_TAILORING_TASK = "Unlock a bolt via Nimble Needlework and craft your bolt cooldown today.",
	PROFACAD_CH_LEATHERWORKING_TITLE = "Leatherworking starter guide",
	PROFACAD_CH_LEATHERWORKING_INTRO = "Leatherworking covers two armour types at once, which is why it has two trees. Decide early which one you actually wear, so your Knowledge goes to the right side.",
	PROFACAD_CH_LEATHERWORKING_BODY = "Leatherworkers are the only source of BOTH crafted leather and mail gear — two separate trees, so pick one armor type first. Learned Leatherworker is the fundamentals tree and even lowers Concentration cost per craft.\n\nStarter build: Lasting Leather (leather) OR Safeguarding Scales (mail) first, maxing one slot sub-node at a time, then Learned Leatherworker. Flawless Fortes comes later: profession tools on one side, consumables (armor kits, drums) on the other — those sell steadily.",
	PROFACAD_CH_LEATHERWORKING_TASK = "Choose leather or mail and max your first slot sub-node.",
	PROFACAD_CH_BLACKSMITHING_TITLE = "Blacksmithing starter guide",
	PROFACAD_CH_BLACKSMITHING_INTRO = "Plate wearers get their gear here, and everyone else comes for stones and tools. Your first step: skill up on the cheap items before you spend any Knowledge.",
	PROFACAD_CH_BLACKSMITHING_BODY = "Blacksmiths are the only source of crafted plate gear, plus weaponstones, tool stones and alloys. The Old Ways is your fundamentals tree (Ingenuity, Multicraft, Resourcefulness for ALL crafts) — feed it alongside whatever you pick.\n\nThen choose a focus: Armorsmithing (split by slot group) or Weaponsmithing (split by weapon type) — max ONE sub-node fully before starting another. Craftsmithing makes profession equipment for six other professions. Alloys, weaponstones and tool stones sell steadily without crafting orders.",
	PROFACAD_CH_BLACKSMITHING_TASK = "Pick your focus tree (Armorsmithing or Weaponsmithing) and max one sub-node first.",
	PROFACAD_CH_ENGINEERING_TITLE = "Engineering starter guide",
	PROFACAD_CH_ENGINEERING_INTRO = "Engineering is the odd, fun one: gadgets, guns and goggles only you can wear. Expect utility and novelty more than raw power.",
	PROFACAD_CH_ENGINEERING_BODY = "Engineers are the sole gun providers and craft goggles (engineer-only), bracers and boots — plus gadgets and scopes. Recycling is the fundamentals tree: skill on ALL crafts, and it returns materials while you work.\n\nAfter Recycling, pick one tree and max a single node first. Heads-up: in Bits and Bots the names mislead — Bits is reagents/cogwheels (scopes), Bots is optional reagents and gadgets. Scopes, boot attachments and gadgets are reliable auction house sellers.",
	PROFACAD_CH_ENGINEERING_TASK = "Put your first points in Recycling, then choose your focus tree.",
	PROFACAD_CH_INSCRIPTION_TITLE = "Inscription starter guide",
	PROFACAD_CH_INSCRIPTION_INTRO = "Inscription is the quiet one: a lot of what scribes make ends up in other players hands rather than their own bags, which is exactly why there is steady demand for it.",
	PROFACAD_CH_INSCRIPTION_BODY = "Scribes craft staves, bows, off-hands, missives (stats on crafted gear), Vantus runes and Darkmoon cards. Unique perk: maxing the central Calm Hands node unlocks the weekly Thalassian Treatise — +2 KP every week, the only profession with that.\n\nSo: Calm Hands first, always. Then Blueprints (gear), Perfected Products (consumables) or Darkmoon Curiosity (cards, split by card type). Vantus runes, missives and inks sell steadily.",
	PROFACAD_CH_INSCRIPTION_TASK = "Work toward maxing the central Calm Hands node (weekly Treatise = +2 KP/week).",
	PROFACAD_CH_JEWELCRAFTING_TITLE = "Jewelcrafting starter guide",
	PROFACAD_CH_JEWELCRAFTING_INTRO = "Rings and necklaces are two slots nobody else can craft, so jewelcrafters never run out of customers.",
	PROFACAD_CH_JEWELCRAFTING_BODY = "Jewelcrafters are the only source of crafted rings and necklaces — everyone needs those. Prospecting turns ore into gems; crushing turns spare gems into Gemdust. Tip: never spend Concentration on prospecting or crushing.\n\nStart with Thoughtful Throughput (fundamentals), then note two shortcuts: only the central Glamorous Gems node is needed to cut Eversong Diamonds at max quality, and Proficient Processor (prospecting/crushing) is cheap to complete. Cut gems and reagents like Sunglass Vial sell well.",
	PROFACAD_CH_JEWELCRAFTING_TASK = "Unlock the central Glamorous Gems node and cut your first quality gem.",
	PROFACAD_CH_HERBALISM_TITLE = "Herbalism starter guide",
	PROFACAD_CH_HERBALISM_INTRO = "Gathering is the calmest way into professions: no recipes to hunt, no tree to plan first, just pick things up. The upgrade everyone wants is gathering without dismounting.",
	PROFACAD_CH_HERBALISM_BODY = "Every herb spawns in every zone, so there is no zone-picking to agonise over. The order below is what the route in this addon now recommends, and each step is one thing you unlock rather than a vague spread.\n\n|cffffcc00First: about 40 points in Botany.|r That is picking while mounted, and it is by far the biggest time saving in the profession — without it you dismount and remount at every single plant. Druids skip this: they already gather mounted.\n\n|cffffcc00Then about 20 in Mulching|r, which people leave out and should not. It gives you Imbued Mulch: a guaranteed rare find on your next pick, and the rare find is Nocturnal Lotus — the only rare-quality Midnight herb, with no node of its own, appearing in all four flasks and both cauldrons.\n\n|cffffcc00Then Bountiful Harvests|r for the long run: more herbs per node and more skill, so top quality more often. Inside it each herb has its own node, so you can aim at whichever sells best on your realm.\n\n|cffffcc00Leave Midnight Overload alone.|r It only works on the elemental nodes, it costs a lot of points, and you meet too few of them. Worth it only if you are farming motes on purpose.\n\nThose point counts come from one source each. Hover the node in game before you click — the tooltip has the real number, and you only get one reset.",
	PROFACAD_CH_HERBALISM_TASK = "Unlock mounted gathering via the Botany root (skip as Druid).",
	PROFACAD_CH_MINING_TITLE = "Mining starter guide",
	PROFACAD_CH_MINING_INTRO = "Mining feeds blacksmithing, jewelcrafting and engineering, so it earns even if you never craft anything yourself. First goal: gathering without dismounting.",
	PROFACAD_CH_MINING_BODY = "All ores appear in all zones. First goal: ~40 points in the Meticulous Mining root to mine while mounted — the biggest quality-of-life unlock in Midnight. Then ~50 in the Plentiful Ores root for skill on every ore.\n\nAfter that, fill individual ore nodes (spread evenly unless one ore is much pricier); maxing an ore boosts Dazzling Thorium drops from it. Special infused deposits (Lightfused, Wild, Primal, Voidbound) can be Overloaded for a pile of motes — first time per ore type grants a Knowledge Point. The 12-hour cooldown drops ~30 minutes with every node you mine, 40 root points add a second charge, and the Over-LODED tree buffs it further. Refulgent Copper is in constant demand.",
	PROFACAD_CH_MINING_TASK = "Unlock mounted mining via the Meticulous Mining root.",
	PROFACAD_CH_SKINNING_TITLE = "Skinning starter guide",
	PROFACAD_CH_SKINNING_INTRO = "Skinning turns things you were killing anyway into materials. It pairs naturally with leatherworking, but it earns perfectly well on its own.",
	PROFACAD_CH_SKINNING_BODY = "Skinners feed leatherworkers — and Midnight gives you tools for it: high-value beasts show on your minimap, Diffusers pull motes from mobs, and Majestic Lures (Talented Tracker) summon a once-per-day zone beast that drops epic materials.\n\nStarter build (farming): a few points in the Thorough Tanning root, then max the leather or scales branch, then back into the root. Next Gainful Gathering: Careful Carving (Finesse, mass farming) or Trophy Taker (Perception, rares). Swap tools to match: Finesse for volume, Perception for rares.",
	PROFACAD_CH_SKINNING_TASK = "Max the leather or scales branch in Thorough Tanning and skin with the matching tool.",
